"A drum has a stretched membrane, called the head.": "42e0e541d2b0",
"A drum has a stretched membrane. When you tighten the head, the pitch goes up. A circular membrane does not vibrate in whole-number multiples. For an ideal drumhead the overtones are about 1.59, 2.14, 2.30, and 2.92 times the lowest frequency. That makes a drum sound different from a string, and many drums have no clear pitch. But some, such as the timpani, are tuned to definite pitches with a pedal that changes the tension of the head.": "80c878eda9aa",
"A drum has overtones that are not whole-number multiples.": "64f88e6a9b9d",
"A guitar string vibrates when you pluck it.": "ca5a32aaf3ae",
"A piano’s soundboard makes the strings sound louder.": "13493275ad36",
"A string’s pitch depends on three things. The shorter the string, the higher the pitch. The tighter the string, the higher the pitch. And the lighter the string, the higher the pitch. A string alone barely moves the air, so most string instruments have a body. The soundboard moves a greater volume of air than the thin string, and that makes the sound louder. The body does not add energy. The string is still where the sound comes from. Strings can be plucked, bowed, or struck.": "447ded114b58",
"A timpanist can tune the drum with a pedal.": "67e3fec8d969",
"Every sound starts with something that vibrates. Musical instruments can be grouped by what it is. A well-known system was devised by Erich von Hornbostel and Curt Sachs and published in 1914. It uses four families: chordophones, where a string vibrates; aerophones, where air vibrates; membranophones, where a stretched membrane vibrates; and idiophones, where the body of the instrument itself vibrates. In Taiwan they are 弦鳴樂器, 氣鳴樂器, 膜鳴樂器, and 體鳴樂器.": "3ba7d322da5f",
"Every sound starts with something that vibrates. Musical instruments can be grouped by what it is. A well-known system was devised by Erich von Hornbostel and Curt Sachs and published in 1914. It uses four families: chordophones, where a string vibrates; aerophones, where air vibrates; membranophones, where a stretched membrane vibrates; and idiophones, where the body of the instrument itself vibrates. In Taiwan they are 弦鳴樂器, 氣鳴樂器, 膜鳴樂器, and 體鳴樂器. A string’s pitch depends on three things. The shorter the string, the higher the pitch. The tighter the string, the higher the pitch. And the lighter the string, the higher the pitch. A string alone barely moves the air, so most string instruments have a body. The soundboard moves a greater volume of air than the thin string, and that makes the sound louder. The body does not add energy. The string is still where the sound comes from. Strings can be plucked, bowed, or struck. In a wind instrument the air inside a tube vibrates, and the player starts it in one of three ways: by blowing across an edge, as in a flute, by making a reed vibrate, as in a clarinet, or by buzzing the lips, as in a trumpet. The pitch depends on the length of the tube. Finger holes, valves, and slides change the effective length. A tube that is closed at one end makes only the odd harmonics, 1, 3, 5, and so on, and sounds an octave lower than an open tube of the same length. A clarinet behaves roughly like that, and a flute behaves like an open tube. A drum has a stretched membrane. When you tighten the head, the pitch goes up. A circular membrane does not vibrate in whole-number multiples. For an ideal drumhead the overtones are about 1.59, 2.14, 2.30, and 2.92 times the lowest frequency. That makes a drum sound different from a string, and many drums have no clear pitch. But some, such as the timpani, are tuned to definite pitches with a pedal that changes the tension of the head. In a marimba or a xylophone, the bar itself vibrates. A free bar has overtones at about 2.76, 5.40, and 8.93 times its lowest frequency, which are not whole numbers either. Makers tune a marimba bar by taking wood from the middle of its underside, which lowers the pitch. The shorter the bar, the higher the pitch. A bell is a body too. A well-tuned bell has a hum, a strike tone, a tierce, a quint, and a nominal. So the family is not about the material. A saxophone is usually made of brass, but it is a woodwind, because a reed starts the vibration. Our ears also use more than the overtones to tell instruments apart. Researchers say the timbre depends mainly on the frequency spectrum, but also on the attack and on how the sound changes in time. The sounds on this page are our own simple recipes, so real instruments sound richer.": "8a0d4699f39f",
"In a marimba or a xylophone, the bar itself vibrates. A free bar has overtones at about 2.76, 5.40, and 8.93 times its lowest frequency, which are not whole numbers either. Makers tune a marimba bar by taking wood from the middle of its underside, which lowers the pitch. The shorter the bar, the higher the pitch. A bell is a body too. A well-tuned bell has a hum, a strike tone, a tierce, a quint, and a nominal.": "9f109ef47bfb",
